---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const moleCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'm', label: 'mass of water (g)', min: 1, max: 100, step: 1, value: 18 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'moles (m ÷ 18.02)', expr: 'm/18.02', unit: 'mol' },
    { label: 'molecules (moles × 6.022e23)', expr: 'm/18.02*6.022e23' },
    { label: 'hydrogen atoms (×2 per molecule)', expr: 'm/18.02*2*6.022e23' },
    { label: 'grams of hydrogen in sample', expr: 'm/18.02*2*1.008', unit: 'g' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/mole-and-stoichiometry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Chemistry's recipes are written in <strong>moles</strong>, not grams — because reactions
    happen molecule-by-molecule, and molecules weigh wildly different amounts. One mole is
    6.022 × 10²³ particles (Avogadro's number), and weighs exactly the formula mass in grams.
    The mole converts the invisible counting world into the weighable kitchen world.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the mole machine — weigh water, count its molecules, and read the atoms hiding inside" widget="sliderLab" config={moleCfg} />

  <p>
    18 g of water is one mole: 6.022 × 10²³ molecules, each with 3 atoms. The readouts show why
    chemists love the unit — mass in, atom counts out, no estimate required. (The number is
    exact by definition since 2019: Avogadro's constant is now fixed at 6.02214076 × 10²³.)
  </p>

  <h2>Stoichiometry: the recipe arithmetic</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Balanced equations are recipes:</strong> 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O reads "2 molecules of hydrogen react with 1 of oxygen to make 2 of water" — and equally "2 moles + 1 mole → 2 moles".</li>
    <li><strong>Find the limiting reagent.</strong> Mix 10 moles H₂ with 10 moles O₂ and hydrogen runs out first (the recipe wants 2:1). Excess oxygen is left over; the H₂ sets the yield.</li>
    <li><strong>Percent yield keeps chemistry honest:</strong> reactions rarely deliver the theoretical maximum — side reactions, losses, and equilibrium (a later lesson) eat into it.</li>
    <li><strong>Empirical formulas come from mass data:</strong> burn a compound, weigh the products, convert to moles, find the whole-number ratio — how the first molecular formulas were discovered.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Stoichiometry); BIPM SI redefinition (2019).</p>
</TopicPage>
